1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:20,530
Autonomy and execution
A goal in plain English. The real model chooses actions; Chromium performs the work.

2
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:27,090
Reliability: observe, then retry
The first save failed. The worker checked the ledger, retried, and verified the exact fields.

3
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:35,810
Verification: saved row and browser evidence
The persisted record matches the source: X-101, USD 1,240.50, due 2026-10-31.

4
00:00:35,810 --> 00:00:54,140
Generalization: a read-only task
A different vendor and objective use the same engine. Reporting makes no ledger write.

5
00:00:54,140 --> 00:01:18,670
Generalization: multiple documents
The same worker records all Company X invoices instead of only the latest one.

6
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:44,390
Product thinking: approval before a high-value write
Northstar's USD 7,800 record requires a human decision. The worker pauses before writing.

7
00:01:44,390 --> 00:02:03,700
Reliability: uncertain acknowledgement
The save commits but its acknowledgement is lost. Checking the ledger prevents a duplicate.

8
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:27,500
Reliability: bounded failure
A permanent outage stops after three save attempts. No false completion claim is made.

9
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:44,230
Verification: reject corrupted persistence
A corrupted amount fails exact-field verification. The conflicting record needs manual review.

10
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:56,470
Product thinking: ask instead of guessing
A missing source due date stops the task. The worker does not invent a value.

11
00:02:56,470 --> 00:03:12,120
Engineering quality and technical understanding
A small planner, executor and verifier. Each component has one job; limitations are explicit.

12
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:23,155
Measured evaluation evidence
Review real API and browser test results. These are observed results in a narrow sandbox, not a universal guarantee.
